From 5caeeacdfb14af89ffc0e750033d3f0b35bd5e81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nan Xiao <nanxstats@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 21:11:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Speed up the integration routines without changing the
 grid or results

Restructure the C routines of the recursive numerical integration so that
the same Jennison & Turnbull grid and Simpson weights give the same results
(agreement with the previous implementation to about 1e-16 in probabilities
and bounds) with less work:

* hupdate(): precompute the scaled previous grid and the normalizing factor
  once per call, so the inner loop over pairs of grid points is one
  subtraction, one multiplication, one exponential and one multiply-add
  (no division per pair). Work storage is a fixed-size stack array as
  elsewhere in the package.
* gsbound1(): the lower-tail crossing probability does not depend on the
  Newton iterate and is now evaluated once per analysis instead of once per
  iteration; the density scale factor is applied once per iteration.
* gsbound(): the density scale factor is hoisted out of the grid loops.
* probrej(): the single-analysis case returns before setting up work
  vectors; shared prototypes live in gsDesign.h instead of per-file extern
  declarations.
* GS_PNORM_LOWER/UPPER macros wrap the normal tail probability; R's pnorm()
  remains the default, -DGS_USE_ERFC selects the C library erfc() (same
  accuracy on platforms with a high quality libm, about 2.5x faster).

Measured on Apple M4 (clang -O2): probrej 20-30% faster, gsbound1 25-35%
faster, gsbound 15% faster, for both one- and two-sided designs. All
regression fixtures pass at tolerance 1e-10.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DJx4n1sdZejSKEf7qvwnWo
